  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations to Washington are $18.5B for FY2026 to date. The state has received 96,721 awards, with an average award size of $191K. Obligations are up 16.9% year over year. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $9.1B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1.5B and an Unknown industry category at $1.1B. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency, obligating $12.4B, and the Department of Energy is the second-largest buyer at $4B. THE BOEING COMPANY is the largest vendor, receiving $9.1B, with BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE and BECHTEL NATIONAL, INC. next at $1.5B and $1.1B respectively. Historical context shows FY21 obligations at $15.6B, FY22 at $12.1B, FY23 at $13B, FY24 at $15.4B, and FY25 at $15.8B. All figures are contract obligations, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal spending in Washington changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are up 16.9% year over year. FY26 to date obligations stand at $18.5B compared with FY25 obligations of $15.8B. The state moved from $12.1B in FY22 and $13B in FY23 to $15.4B in FY24 before reaching the current-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal obligations in Washington?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $12.4B and the Department of Energy obligated $4B. THE BOEING COMPANY received $9.1B, and BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE received $1.5B. Aircraft Manufacturing is the top industry at $9.1B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1.5B."
      }
    ]
